#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "Path-Walk API"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:6
msgid ""
"The path-walk API is used to walk reachable objects, but to visit objects in "
"batches based on a common path they appear in, or by type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:12
msgid ""
"For example, all reachable commits are visited in a group. All tags are "
"visited in a group. Then, all root trees are visited. At some point, all "
"blobs reachable via a path `my/dir/to/A` are visited. When there are "
"multiple paths possible to reach the same object, then only one of those "
"paths is used to visit the object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:20
msgid ""
"To use the path-walk API, include `path-walk.h` and call "
"`walk_objects_by_path()` with a customized `path_walk_info` struct. The "
"struct is used to set all of the options for how the walk should proceed.  "
"Let's dig into the different options and their use."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:21
#, no-wrap
msgid "`path_fn` and `path_fn_data`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:28
msgid ""
"The most important option is the `path_fn` option, which is a function "
"pointer to the callback that can execute logic on the object IDs for objects "
"grouped by type and path. This function also receives a `data` value that "
"corresponds to the `path_fn_data` member, for providing custom data "
"structures to this callback function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "`revs`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:36
msgid ""
"To configure the exact details of the reachable set of objects, use the "
"`revs` member and initialize it using the revision machinery in `revision."
"h`. Initialize `revs` using calls such as `setup_revisions()` or "
"`parse_revision_opt()`. Do not call `prepare_revision_walk()`, as that will "
"be called within `walk_objects_by_path()`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:41
msgid ""
"It is also important that you do not specify the `--objects` flag for the "
"`revs` struct. The revision walk should only be used to walk commits, and "
"the objects will be walked in a separate way based on those starting commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "`commits`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:43
#, no-wrap
msgid "`blobs`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:44
#, no-wrap
msgid "`trees`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "`tags`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:50
msgid ""
"By default, these members are enabled and signal that the path-walk API "
"should call the `path_fn` on objects of these types. Specialized "
"applications could disable some options to make it simpler to walk the "
"objects or to have fewer calls to `path_fn`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:53
msgid ""
"While it is possible to walk only commits in this way, consumers would be "
"better off using the revision walk API instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "`prune_all_uninteresting`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:61
msgid ""
"By default, all reachable paths are emitted by the path-walk API.  This "
"option allows consumers to declare that they are not interested in paths "
"where all included objects are marked with the `UNINTERESTING` flag. This "
"requires using the `boundary` option in the revision walk so that the walk "
"emits commits marked with the `UNINTERESTING` flag."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "`edge_aggressive`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:69
msgid ""
"For performance reasons, usually only the boundary commits are explored to "
"find UNINTERESTING objects. However, in the case of shallow clones it can be "
"helpful to mark all trees and blobs reachable from UNINTERESTING tip commits "
"as UNINTERESTING. This matches the behavior of `--objects-edge-aggressive` "
"in the revision API."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "`pl`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/api-path-walk.adoc:77
msgid ""
"This pattern list pointer allows focusing the path-walk search to a set of "
"patterns, only emitting paths that match the given patterns. See linkgit:"
"gitignore[5] or linkgit:git-sparse-checkout[1] for details about pattern "
"lists.  When the pattern list uses cone-mode patterns, then the path-walk "
"API can prune the set of paths it walks to improve performance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
